abstract: |
  The Gaia DR3 catalog of over one million wide binaries opens a precise window onto gravity in the weak-field regime (a ≲ 10⁻¹⁰ m s⁻²), yet whether the observed velocity excess reflects modified gravity or unresolved systematics remains contested. In the Temporal Equivalence Principle (TEP), a conformal scalar field modulates matter proper time as dτ/dt ≈ A(ϕ), with A(ϕ) = exp(β_A ϕ/M_Pl). The Cepheid-calibrated response scale is denoted κ_Cep, while the wide-binary transition is parameterized independently by the velocity-profile saturation amplitude α_sat, not by a bare scalar coupling. This paper tests whether the Gaia wide-binary anomaly is better described as smooth Temporal Shear recovery in weak-field environments. From 341,315 high-purity systems, the analysis identifies a screening transition at R_s = 2,646 ± 182 AU (statistical; ± 609 AU total), strongly preferred over both a flat Newtonian profile (Δ χ² = 14,845) and a constant boost (Δ χ² = 3,583). The transition is strongly preferred under diagonal, AR(1) and Gaussian-process covariance treatments; even the most conservative covariance-aware comparison gives Δχ² ≥ 1,073 against a flat Newtonian profile and Δχ² ≥ 284 against a constant boost. At large separation the profile saturates at α_sat = 0.366 ± 0.012, roughly 35–40% above the Keplerian baseline. Broader smooth-transition fits preserve the same few-thousand-AU onset. The signal also shows the environmental ordering required by TEP. With a non-circular metallicity guardrail that uses a conservative external β_MLR prior unless independent spectroscopic metallicities are cached, the lower-density high-|Z| population transitions at smaller radius than the higher-density midplane. The headline comparison is the joint profile-likelihood fit, which shares one saturation amplitude across environments—the physically prescribed constraint, since screening modulates the recovery scale while the ambient-floor-limited plateau is a shared response at fixed environment—and returns R_s = 3,447 ± 366 versus 4,912 ± 792 AU with Δχ² = 60.3 (p = 8.1 × 10⁻¹⁵), the ordering preserved in 100% of resamples. The model-free permutation test confirms the ordering at p < 10⁻⁴ for s > 3 kAU, and is unchanged with no purity cut applied at all. Under bound-probability weighting the profiles rise monotonically to the widest measured separations, the absolute high- versus low-|Z| contrast grows to +0.094 ± 0.018 (5.3σ) at s = 15–30 kAU, and a mixture deconvolution leaves the bound-component median elevated at s = 20–50 kAU under interloper fractions up to three times the prior estimate. Scrambling tests and phase-mixed Newtonian orbital forward models fail to reproduce the observed screening preference. The wide-binary anomaly is therefore not a generic low-acceleration excess but a structured, environmentally modulated screening transition—one whose morphology, onset scale, and environmental ordering are quantitatively consistent with the conformal scalar field of TEP and are not reproduced by the Newtonian orbital-projection or MOND/EFE parameterizations tested here.
title: 'Temporal Equivalence Principle: Temporal Shear Recovery in Gaia DR3 Wide Binaries'
